    "lem-nrqmx-bloch-fiber-model-properties": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-fourier-basis-and-parseval-on-the-n-torus",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $n\\ge1$\nand let $e_k(x)=\\exp(2\\pi i\\,k\\cdot x)$, $k\\in\\mathbb Z^n$, be the coordinate\ncharacters on $\\mathbb T^n$\n([[def-the-one-dimensional-torus-and-normalized-haar-integral]],\n[[def-fourier-coefficients-and-trigonometric-polynomials]]). Then:",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-parseval-equivalences-for-a-complete-orthonormal-family",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let\n$(e_i)_{i\\in I}$ be an orthonormal family in a real or complex Hilbert space $H$\n([[def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis]]), and\nfor $x\\in H$ and finite $F\\subseteq I$ put",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-complex-spectral-theorem-for-normal-endomorphisms",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional complex inner product space and let $T:V\\to V$\nbe linear. Then $T$ is normal if and only if $V$ has an orthonormal basis\nconsisting of eigenvectors of $T$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.4",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-cauchy-integral-formula-circle",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a\\in\\mathbb C$, let $R>0$, and let $f$ be holomorphic on the disc",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For finitely supported $\\psi$, each shift $\\psi_n\\mapsto\\psi_{n-\\delta}$ preserves the $\\ell^2$ norm, so the triangle inequality gives $\\|H_0\\psi\\|_2\\le(\\sum_{\\delta\\in S}\\|T_\\delta\\|)\\|\\psi\\|_2$. Hence $H_0$ extends uniquely to a bounded operator $H$. For finitely supported $\\psi,\\phi$, reindexing the finite sum gives $\\langle H_0\\psi,\\phi\\rangle=\\sum_{m,\\delta}\\langle T_\\delta\\psi_m,\\phi_{m+\\delta}\\rangle=\\sum_{m,\\delta}\\langle\\psi_m,T_\\delta^*\\phi_{m+\\delta}\\rangle=\\langle\\psi,H_0\\phi\\rangle$, because $T_\\delta^*=T_{-\\delta}$ and $S=-S$. Density and boundedness extend symmetry to all of $\\mathcal H$, so $H=H^*$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Under $k=2\\pi x$, the functions $e^{-ik\\cdot n}$ are the characters $e_{-n}(x)$. By [F1] and [F2], applied to each of the finitely many internal coordinates, $\\mathcal F_0$ preserves the $L^2$ norm and its range contains the dense trigonometric polynomials; it therefore has a unique isometric extension $\\mathcal F$, and the completeness clause of [F1] makes that extension onto. Thus $\\mathcal F$ is unitary.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "Direct substitution and the change of index $m=n-\\delta$ give, for finitely supported $\\psi$, $\\mathcal F_0(H_0\\psi)(k)=\\sum_{\\delta\\in S}T_\\delta e^{-ik\\cdot\\delta}\\sum_m\\psi_m e^{-ik\\cdot m}=h(k)\\mathcal F_0\\psi(k)$. The finite sum defining $h$ is continuous and satisfies $\\sup_k\\|h(k)\\|\\le\\sum_{\\delta\\in S}\\|T_\\delta\\|$, so multiplication by $h$ is bounded on $L^2$. Extending the identity from the dense finitely supported sequences proves the claimed unitary equivalence. Also, $h(k)^*=\\sum_\\delta T_\\delta^*e^{ik\\cdot\\delta}=\\sum_\\delta T_{-\\delta}e^{-ik\\cdot(-\\delta)}=h(k)$, and its entries are trigonometric polynomials, hence smooth.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-4",
          "claim": "Write the eigenvalues of $h(k)$ in nondecreasing order as $\\lambda_1(k)\\le\\cdots\\le\\lambda_r(k)$. For a Hermitian matrix $A$ with eigenbasis $e_1,\\ldots,e_r$, its $j$th eigenvalue satisfies $\\lambda_j(A)=\\max_{\\dim L=r-j+1}\\min_{\\|v\\|=1,\\,v\\in L}\\langle Av,v\\rangle$: every such $L$ intersects $\\operatorname{span}(e_1,\\ldots,e_j)$ nontrivially, while $L=\\operatorname{span}(e_j,\\ldots,e_r)$ attains the value. Since every unit-vector Rayleigh quotient changes by at most $\\|A-B\\|$ when $A$ is replaced by $B$, this formula gives $|\\lambda_j(A)-\\lambda_j(B)|\\le\\|A-B\\|$. Thus each $\\lambda_j(k)$ is continuous.",
          "step": "1.4",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Fix $k_0$. The gap and continuity in step 1.4 let us choose disjoint small circles around the distinct eigenvalues in the selected block at $k_0$ so that, on some neighborhood of $k_0$, all selected eigenvalues stay inside their union and all complementary eigenvalues stay outside. The inverse matrix $(zI-h(k))^{-1}$ is $C^\\infty$ in $k$ on this fixed contour: differentiating the inverse gives $\\partial_j(zI-h)^{-1}=(zI-h)^{-1}(\\partial_jh)(zI-h)^{-1}$, and repeated differentiation gives finite sums of products of smooth matrices. Therefore its contour integral is $C^\\infty$. At each $k$, [F3] diagonalizes $h(k)$, and [F4] evaluates the contour integral on each eigenvector, proving that it is exactly the orthogonal projector onto the selected eigenvalues. On overlaps the local definitions project onto the same selected eigenspaces, so they agree and define the asserted global projector.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.4",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
