    "lem-nrqmx-degree-area-formula": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "thm-de-rham-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. For every finite-dimensional Hausdorff second-countable smooth manifold $M$, possibly with boundary, integration on smooth simplices followed by the inverse of restriction from continuous to smooth singular cohomology gives a natural isomorphism of unital graded real algebras\n$$J_M:H^*_{\\mathrm{dR}}(M)\\xrightarrow{\\cong}H^*_{\\mathrm{sing}}(M;\\mathbb R).$$\nThe source product is wedge of form classes, the target product is the front/back singular cup product, and both stars denote direct sums of the homogeneous groups. Naturality is for smooth maps. No compactness assumption is imposed.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-degree-of-a-map-between-oriented-closed-manifolds",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $f:M\\to N$ be continuous, where $M,N$ are **nonempty connected closed integrally oriented $n$-manifolds** of the same dimension. Closed means compact and boundaryless. Write $[M],[N]$ for their specified fundamental classes from [[def-fundamental-class-of-a-compact-oriented-manifold]]. The **degree** of $f$ is the integer $d=\\deg(f)$ determined by\n$$f_*[M]=d[N]\\quad\\text{in }H_n(N;\\mathbb Z).$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-fundamental-class-of-a-compact-oriented-manifold",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a compact boundaryless $n$-manifold with a specified $R$-orientation $\\mu$, where $R$ is a commutative unital ring. Its **fundamental class** is\n$$[M]=[M]_M\\in H_n(M,M\\setminus M;R)=H_n(M;R),$$\nusing [[lem-compatible-local-orientation-classes-exist-over-compact-subsets]] with the compact support $K=M$. The final equality holds at the chain-complex level: $C_*(\\varnothing;R)=0$, so quotienting by it leaves $C_*(M;R)$. The cited lemma proves existence and uniqueness of this class with point restriction $\\mu_x$ at every $x\\in M$. Thus the notation denotes a class determined by the supplied orientation, not an arbitrary generator chosen afterward.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-de-rham-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. For every finite-dimensional Hausdorff second-countable smooth manifold $M$, possibly with boundary, integration on smooth simplices followed by the inverse of restriction from continuous to smooth singular cohomology gives a natural isomorphism of unital graded real algebras\n$$J_M:H^*_{\\mathrm{dR}}(M)\\xrightarrow{\\cong}H^*_{\\mathrm{sing}}(M;\\mathbb R).$$\nThe source product is wedge of form classes, the target product is the front/back singular cup product, and both stars denote direct sums of the homogeneous groups. Naturality is for smooth maps. No compactness assumption is imposed.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-kronecker-evaluation-pairing",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a space $X$, abelian group $G$ and integer $n\\ge0$. For a singular cocycle $\\varphi\\in Z^n(X;G)$ and an integral singular cycle $c\\in Z_n(X;\\mathbb Z)$, define their **evaluation** to be $\\varphi(c)\\in G$, using [[def-singular-cohomology-with-coefficients]] and [[def-singular-chain-complex-and-singular-homology]]. If $c=\\sum_\\sigma a_\\sigma\\sigma$ is its finite formal expansion, this value is $\\sum_\\sigma a_\\sigma\\varphi(\\sigma)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-the-kronecker-pairing-is-independent-of-cocycle-and-cycle-representatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The Kronecker rule $\\langle[\\varphi],[c]\\rangle=\\varphi(c)$ is independent of both cocycle and cycle representatives and is additive in both variables. For a continuous $f:X\\to Y$, $\\alpha\\in H^n(Y;G)$ and $z\\in H_n(X;\\mathbb Z)$, it satisfies\n$$\\langle f^*\\alpha,z\\rangle=\\langle\\alpha,f_*z\\rangle.$$\nFor a coefficient homomorphism $u:G\\to G'$, it satisfies $\\langle u_*\\alpha,z\\rangle=u\\langle\\alpha,z\\rangle$. The same representative-independence argument gives the $R$-bilinear pairing for $R$-linear cochains on chains over a commutative ring $R$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-de-rham-top-integral-is-fundamental-class-evaluation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $M$ be a compact oriented smooth boundaryless\n$m$-manifold and let $\\alpha\\in\\Omega^m(M)$ be a smooth real or complex top form.\nLet $J_M:H^m_{\\mathrm{dR}}(M)\\to H^m_{\\mathrm{sing}}(M;\\mathbb R)$ be the de Rham\ncomparison of [[thm-de-rham-theorem]] for real forms, extended\ncomplex-linearly with codomain $H^m_{\\mathrm{sing}}(M;\\mathbb C)$ for complex\nforms. Use the smooth-orientation normalization: a positively oriented affine\n$m$-simplex represents the positive local homology generator at each interior\npoint. Let $[M]\\in H_m(M;\\mathbb R)$ be the real fundamental class of\n[[def-fundamental-class-of-a-compact-oriented-manifold]], and let\n$\\langle\\,,\\rangle$ be the Kronecker evaluation pairing\n([[def-kronecker-evaluation-pairing]]). Then",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-singular-chain-complex-and-singular-homology",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a topological space $X$ and an abelian group $G$, the singular chain groups\nand boundary maps of [[def-singular-boundary-operator]] form the\n**singular chain complex**\n$$C_\\bullet(X;G):=\\bigl(\\cdots\\to C_n(X;G)\\xrightarrow{\\partial_n}C_{n-1}(X;G)\\to\\cdots\\bigr),$$\nbecause [[thm-the-singular-boundary-squares-to-zero]] gives\n$\\partial_{n-1}\\partial_n=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-induced-singular-chain-map",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $f:X\\to Y$ be a continuous map of topological spaces. For each $n\\geq 0$,\nthe **induced singular chain map in degree $n$** is the homomorphism\n$$f_{\\#,n}:C_n(X;\\mathbb Z)\\to C_n(Y;\\mathbb Z)$$\ndefined on a singular simplex $\\sigma:\\Delta^n\\to X$ by\n$$f_{\\#,n}(\\sigma):=f\\circ\\sigma,$$\nand extended $\\mathbb Z$-linearly.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "prop-singular-chains-and-homology-are-covariantly-functorial",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For each abelian group $G$, the assignments\n$$X\\mapsto C_\\bullet(X;G),\\qquad f\\mapsto f_\\#$$\nand\n$$X\\mapsto H_n^{\\mathrm{sing}}(X;G),\\qquad f\\mapsto H_n(f_\\#)$$\ndefine covariant functors from topological spaces to chain complexes and to\nabelian groups, respectively. Equivalently,\n$$\\operatorname{id}_{X,\\#}=\\operatorname{id}_{C_\\bullet(X;G)},\\qquad (g\\circ f)_\\#=g_\\#\\circ f_\\#,$$\nand for every $n\\geq 0$,\n$$H_n((g\\circ f)_\\#)=H_n(g_\\#)\\circ H_n(f_\\#),\\qquad H_n(\\operatorname{id}_{X,\\#})=\\operatorname{id}_{H_n(X;G)}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "In spherical coordinates $x=\\sin\\theta\\cos\\phi$, $y=\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi$, $z=\\cos\\theta$, the unnormalized displayed two-form is $\\sin\\theta\\,d\\theta\\wedge d\\phi$. Integrating for $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi$ and $0\\le\\phi\\le2\\pi$ gives $4\\pi$, so $\\omega_{S^2}$ is a smooth closed form with integral one; its displayed ambient expression shows it extends smoothly across the coordinate poles.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Let $J$ be the natural comparison in [F3]. By [F5], $\\int_{\\mathbb T^2}n^*\\omega_{S^2}=\\langle J([n^*\\omega_{S^2}]),[\\mathbb T^2]_{\\mathbb R}\\rangle$. Naturality of $J$ and of the real-coefficient Kronecker pairing [F4] then gives $\\int_{\\mathbb T^2}n^*\\omega_{S^2}=\\langle J([\\omega_{S^2}]),n_*[\\mathbb T^2]_{\\mathbb R}\\rangle$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "By [F1], $n_*[\\mathbb T^2]_{\\mathbb Z}=\\deg(n)[S^2]_{\\mathbb Z}$. The coefficient map $\\iota:C_\\bullet(X;\\mathbb Z)\\to C_\\bullet(X;\\mathbb R)$ sends each finite sum $\\sum_j a_j\\sigma_j$ to $\\sum_j a_j\\sigma_j$ with the same coefficients viewed in $\\mathbb R$. It commutes with boundaries because [F6] defines the singular boundary as an integer linear combination of faces and $\\iota$ is additive; it commutes with $n_\\#$ because [F6] postcomposes each simplex without changing its coefficient. Hence it induces a coefficient-change map on homology and carries each integral fundamental class to its real fundamental class: it sends the positive local orientation generator $1$ to $1$, so uniqueness of the class with those local restrictions in [F2] identifies the image. Applying $\\iota$ to the degree equation gives $n_*[\\mathbb T^2]_{\\mathbb R}=\\deg(n)[S^2]_{\\mathbb R}$. By the real-coefficient bilinearity of [F4], step 2.1 becomes $\\int_{\\mathbb T^2}n^*\\omega_{S^2}=\\deg(n)\\langle J([\\omega_{S^2}]),[S^2]_{\\mathbb R}\\rangle=\\deg(n)\\int_{S^2}\\omega_{S^2}=\\deg(n)$ by [F5] and step 1.1.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
