    "thm-nrqmx-bloch-projector-chern": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-determinant-line-projector",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a smooth manifold, let $r\\ge1$, and let $P:M\\to M_r(\\mathbb C)$ be a $C^2$ orthogonal projector of constant rank $m$, with $1\\le m\\le r$. Its image bundle is $E_P=\\{(x,v):P(x)v=v\\}$, with the projected connection $\\nabla s=P\\,ds$ of [[def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle]]. The **determinant line bundle** is the complex line bundle",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-determinant-line-projector-well-defined",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For the projector of [[def-nrqmx-determinant-line-projector]], $Q(x)=\\bigwedge^mP(x)$ is a $C^2$ orthogonal projector of rank one. Its range is exactly $\\det E_P(x)=\\bigwedge^m\\operatorname{im}P(x)$, so these ranges form the determinant line bundle.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-determinant-curvature-is-trace",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $P$ be a $C^2$ rank-$m$ orthogonal projector and let $F=P(dP\\wedge dP)P$ be the curvature of its projected connection. On any open set with a $C^2$ orthonormal frame $V=(v_1,\\ldots,v_m)$, put $A=V^*dV$ and $w=v_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge v_m$. The induced projected connection on $\\det E_P$ has local connection one-form $a=\\operatorname{tr}A$ in the unit frame $w$, and its scalar curvature is",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-nrqmx-rank-one-chern-holonomy",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $\\Sigma$ be a compact boundaryless oriented smooth surface, and let $P:\\Sigma\\to\\operatorname{End}(\\mathbb C^d)$ be a $C^2$ rank-one orthogonal projection family. Let $E_P$ be its complex line bundle, $\\nabla=P\\,d$, and $F=P(dP\\wedge dP)P$. Then\n$$\\frac{i}{2\\pi}\\int_\\Sigma\\operatorname{tr}F=\\langle c_1(E_P),[\\Sigma]\\rangle\\in\\mathbb Z.$$\nFor any $C^2$ closed path $\\gamma$ subdivided into local unit frames $u_j$, with $A_j=u_j^*du_j$ and transition $u_{j+1}=u_jg_j$ for $g_j$ valued in $U(1)$ at the crossing points, the holonomy is the scalar by which parallel transport acts on its one-dimensional initial fiber. This scalar is the product of the local factors $\\exp(-\\int_{\\gamma_j}A_j)$ and the transition factors $g_j^{-1}$. Thus geometric holonomy includes the frame-transition scalars whenever one frame does not cover the path.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-c1-stokes-compact-surface",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $M$ be a compact oriented smooth surface with boundary, and let $D\\subseteq M$ be a compact oriented region whose boundary consists of finitely many $C^2$ arcs meeting at finitely many corners. Let $\\eta=a\\,dx+b\\,dy$ be a complex-valued $C^1$ one-form on a neighborhood of $D$ in each local chart, where $C^1$ means its coefficients and first partial derivatives are continuous up to the boundary. Define $d\\eta=(\\partial_xb-\\partial_ya)\\,dx\\wedge dy$ in each chart; the chain rule and symmetry of second derivatives make this expression coordinate-independent. Give $\\partial D$ the outward-normal-first orientation. Then\n$$\\int_D d\\eta=\\int_{\\partial D}\\eta.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-chern-classes-from-the-projective-bundle-relation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $E\\to B$ be a numerable complex rank-$n$ bundle with $n\\geq1$\nover a path-connected CW complex $B$ (or over one of the CW-type bases of\n[[thm-integral-complex-projective-bundle-theorem]]). By that theorem there are\nunique classes $a_i\\in H^{2i}(B;\\mathbb Z)$, $1\\leq i\\leq n$, such that\n$$x_E^{\\,n}-a_1x_E^{\\,n-1}+a_2x_E^{\\,n-2}-\\cdots+(-1)^na_n=0\\qquad\\text{in }H^{2n}(P(E);\\mathbb Z),$$\nwhere $x_E\\in H^2(P(E);\\mathbb Z)$ is the Euler class of the underlying real\nbundle of the tautological line\n([[def-complex-projective-bundle-and-tautological-complex-line]]). The\n**Chern classes** of $E$ are these coefficients:\n$$c_i(E):=a_i\\in H^{2i}(B;\\mathbb Z)\\qquad(1\\leq i\\leq n),$$\ncompleted by the conventions $c_0(E):=1\\in H^0(B;\\mathbb Z)$ and\n$c_i(E):=0$ for $i>n$, and the **total Chern class** is the finite sum\n$c(E):=\\sum_{i\\geq0}c_i(E)=1+c_1(E)+\\cdots+c_n(E)\\in H^*(B;\\mathbb Z)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-bloch-fiber-model-properties",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and the data of [[def-nrqmx-bloch-fiber-model]]. The finite-range operator $H_0$ extends uniquely to a bounded self-adjoint operator $H$ on $\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z^d;\\mathbb C^r)$, and $\\mathcal F_0$ extends uniquely to a unitary map $\\mathcal F:\\mathcal H\\to L^2(\\mathbb T^d_{2\\pi};\\mathbb C^r)$. For every $k$, $h(k)$ is Hermitian, $\\|h(k)\\|\\le\\sum_{\\delta\\in S}\\|T_\\delta\\|$, and",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For each fixed $t$ (or for the single projector), [F1] gives the rank-one projector $Q_t=\\bigwedge^mP_t$ onto $\\det E_{P_t}$. Its image is a complex line bundle over the compact torus, hence is numerable; the torus is a compact smooth CW complex. The determinant-line curvature lemma [F2] gives $\\operatorname{tr}F_t=\\operatorname{tr}(Q_t(dQ_t\\wedge dQ_t)Q_t)$ pointwise, with the same orientation convention. Applying [F3] to $Q_t$ proves the displayed equality and integrality, with $c_1$ interpreted by [F5].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For a jointly $C^2$ family, the coefficient function $g(k,t)$ of the two-form $\\operatorname{tr}(P_t(dP_t\\wedge dP_t)P_t)$ is continuous on the compact set $\\mathbb T^2\\times[0,1]$, so it is uniformly continuous. If $t_j\\to t$, then $\\sup_k|g(k,t_j)-g(k,t)|\\to0$; therefore $C(P_{t_j})\\to C(P_t)$ by bounding the difference of integrals by the torus's finite area times this supremum. Thus $t\\mapsto C(P_t)$ is continuous. Step 1.1 makes it integer-valued, and a continuous integer-valued function on the connected interval $[0,1]$ is constant: otherwise the intermediate-value theorem would give a noninteger value between two distinct integer values. For a uniformly gapped Hamiltonian deformation, [F6] supplies the required joint $C^2$ spectral projectors by the local contour formula.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "If $V$ is a global $C^2$ periodic orthonormal frame, then $A=V^*dV$ is a globally defined $C^1$ matrix-valued one-form. By [F2] and its proof, $\\operatorname{tr}F=d\\operatorname{tr}A$ (the trace of $A\\wedge A$ vanishes). Hence $C(P)=\\frac{i}{2\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb T^2}d\\operatorname{tr}A=0$ by [F4], since the torus has empty boundary.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "given"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
