    "thm-nrqmx-two-band-degree-formula": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-bloch-fiber-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $d,r\\ge1$ be integers, let $S\\subset\\mathbb Z^d$ be finite with $S=-S$, and let $T_\\delta\\in M_r(\\mathbb C)$ satisfy $T_{-\\delta}=T_\\delta^*$ for each $\\delta\\in S$. Give $\\mathbb C^r$ its standard Hermitian inner product, conjugate-linear in the first variable, and set $\\mathcal H=\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z^d;\\mathbb C^r)$ with the sum inner product. On finitely supported $\\psi\\in\\mathcal H$ define",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-bloch-fiber-model-properties",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and the data of [[def-nrqmx-bloch-fiber-model]]. The finite-range operator $H_0$ extends uniquely to a bounded self-adjoint operator $H$ on $\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z^d;\\mathbb C^r)$, and $\\mathcal F_0$ extends uniquely to a unitary map $\\mathcal F:\\mathcal H\\to L^2(\\mathbb T^d_{2\\pi};\\mathbb C^r)$. For every $k$, $h(k)$ is Hermitian, $\\|h(k)\\|\\le\\sum_{\\delta\\in S}\\|T_\\delta\\|$, and",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-nrqmx-bloch-projector-chern",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $P:\\mathbb T^2_{2\\pi}\\to M_r(\\mathbb C)$ be a $C^2$ orthogonal projector of constant rank $m\\ge1$; in particular it may be the projector of an isolated band cluster of a finite-range Bloch Hamiltonian. Use the orientation $dk_1\\wedge dk_2$, let $E_P=\\operatorname{im}P$, and let $F=P(dP\\wedge dP)P$ be the curvature of the projected connection $\\nabla=P\\,d$. Then",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-degree-area-formula",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Orient the unit sphere $S^2\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ outward and let $\\omega_{S^2}$ be its normalized area form",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Direct multiplication of the displayed Pauli matrices gives $\\sigma_a\\sigma_b=\\delta_{ab}I+i\\sum_c\\epsilon_{abc}\\sigma_c$, $\\operatorname{tr}\\sigma_a=0$, and $\\operatorname{tr}(\\sigma_a\\sigma_b)=2\\delta_{ab}$. Thus $(n\\cdot\\sigma)^2=|n|^2I=I$, and $h=d_0I+|d|(n\\cdot\\sigma)$ has eigenvalues $d_0\\pm|d|$ with the stated orthogonal projectors. Since $|d|$ is continuous and positive on compact $\\mathbb T^2$, its minimum is positive, so the two bands are uniformly separated; $n$ and $P_s$ are smooth.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Differentiating $P_s=(I+s n\\cdot\\sigma)/2$ gives $\\partial_jP_s=(s/2)(\\partial_jn)\\cdot\\sigma$. The Pauli product identity yields $[a\\cdot\\sigma,b\\cdot\\sigma]=2i(a\\times b)\\cdot\\sigma$, hence $[\\partial_1P_s,\\partial_2P_s]=\\frac{i}{2}(\\partial_1n\\times\\partial_2n)\\cdot\\sigma$. Using $\\operatorname{tr}(P_s\\sigma_a)=s n_a$, which follows from the trace identities in step 1.1, we obtain $\\operatorname{tr}(P_s[\\partial_1P_s,\\partial_2P_s])=\\frac{i s}{2}n\\cdot(\\partial_1n\\times\\partial_2n)$. In dimension two this is the coefficient of $\\operatorname{tr}(P_s(dP_s\\wedge dP_s)P_s)$: cyclicity and $P_s^2=P_s$ give $\\operatorname{tr}(P_sXP_s)=\\operatorname{tr}(P_sX)$. Multiplying by $i/(2\\pi)$ therefore gives $C(P_s)=-s(4\\pi)^{-1}\\int n\\cdot(\\partial_1n\\times\\partial_2n)\\,dk_1dk_2$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The normalized area form in [F3] pulls back to $(4\\pi)^{-1}n\\cdot(\\partial_1n\\times\\partial_2n)\\,dk_1\\wedge dk_2$, by the defining outward area form on the unit sphere. Hence its integral is $\\deg(n)$ by [F3]. Substitution into step 2.1 gives $C(P_s)=-s\\deg(n)$, which is the two displayed upper- and lower-band formulas.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement assumes d nowhere zero, excluding gap-closing points."
        },
        {
