          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and the data of [[def-nrqmx-bloch-fiber-model]]. The finite-range operator $H_0$ extends uniquely to a bounded self-adjoint operator $H$ on $\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z^d;\\mathbb C^r)$, and $\\mathcal F_0$ extends uniquely to a unitary map $\\mathcal F:\\mathcal H\\to L^2(\\mathbb T^d_{2\\pi};\\mathbb C^r)$. For every $k$, $h(k)$ is Hermitian, $\\|h(k)\\|\\le\\sum_{\\delta\\in S}\\|T_\\delta\\|$, and",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-nrqmx-two-band-degree-formula",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let a two-orbital finite-range Bloch Hamiltonian on the oriented torus $\\mathbb T^2_{2\\pi}$ have the form",
          "uses": [
            "2.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Differentiating $u^*u=1$ gives $(\\partial_j u)^*u+u^*\\partial_j u=0$, so $u^*\\partial_j u$ is purely imaginary and $\\mathcal A_j=i u^*\\partial_j u$ is real. If only the smooth rank-one projector $P(k)$ is given, fix a unit vector $v\\in\\operatorname{im}P(k_0)$; by continuity $P(k)v\\ne0$ near $k_0$, and $u(k)=P(k)v/\\|P(k)v\\|$ supplies the required local $C^2$ normalized section. Under $u'=e^{i\\chi}u$, direct differentiation gives $\\mathcal A'=\\mathcal A-\\nabla_k\\chi$, so $\\boldsymbol\\Omega'=\\boldsymbol\\Omega$ and the Lagrangian changes by $-\\hbar\\,d\\chi/dt$, a total derivative. Thus the equations are chart independent.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For each coordinate $x_j$, $\\partial L/\\partial\\dot x_j=\\hbar k_j$ and $\\partial L/\\partial x_j=q(E_{\\rm ext})_j$. The Euler–Lagrange equation $\\frac{d}{dt}(\\partial L/\\partial\\dot x_j)-\\partial L/\\partial x_j=0$ therefore gives $\\hbar\\dot k_j=q(E_{\\rm ext})_j$, the first vector equation.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "For each $k_j$, $\\partial L/\\partial\\dot k_j=\\hbar\\mathcal A_j$ and $\\partial L/\\partial k_j=\\hbar\\dot x_j+\\hbar\\sum_i(\\partial_j\\mathcal A_i)\\dot k_i-\\partial_j\\varepsilon$. Applying Euler–Lagrange and the chain rule gives $\\hbar\\dot x_j=\\partial_j\\varepsilon+\\hbar\\sum_i(\\partial_i\\mathcal A_j-\\partial_j\\mathcal A_i)\\dot k_i=\\partial_j\\varepsilon-\\hbar(\\dot k\\times\\boldsymbol\\Omega)_j$, where the last equality is the component identity $\\partial_i\\mathcal A_j-\\partial_j\\mathcal A_i=-\\epsilon_{j i\\ell}(\\nabla_k\\times\\mathcal A)_\\ell$. Dividing by $\\hbar>0$ gives the second vector equation.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-3",
          "claim": "On a two-band chart with $P_s=u u^*$, direct differentiation of $P_s$ gives $\\Omega_{12}=i\\,\\operatorname{tr}(P_s[\\partial_1P_s,\\partial_2P_s])$. Thus $(2\\pi)^{-1}\\int\\Omega_{12}=C(P_s)$ in the projector convention, and [F2] gives its signed degree value; this confirms that the anomalous term uses the same sign convention as the preceding band formula. The variational derivation itself does not depend on a two-band restriction.",
          "step": "2.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The result is the Euler–Lagrange consequence of the adopted finite-dimensional effective Lagrangian. It does not establish that a physical wave packet follows these equations, nor quantify any error from neglected interband transitions or higher spatial and temporal derivatives of the field.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "step 2.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
