    "ex-nrqmx-two-band-gap-closures": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-nrqmx-two-band-degree-formula",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let a two-orbital finite-range Bloch Hamiltonian on the oriented torus $\\mathbb T^2_{2\\pi}$ have the form",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-regular-value-formula-for-degree",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $F:M^n\\to N^n$ be proper and smooth between nonempty connected oriented smooth manifolds without boundary, and let $y\\in N$ be a regular value. Then the fibre is finite and\n$$\\deg(F)=\\sum_{p\\in F^{-1}(y)}\\operatorname{sgn}(dF_p)\\in\\mathbb Z.$$\nIf $M,N$ are closed, this scalar is also their integral homological degree: with the integral orientations induced by the supplied smooth orientations using the same Euclidean generator convention,\n$$F_*[M]=\\deg(F)[N]\\quad\\text{in }H_n(N;\\mathbb Z).$$\nThe formula includes the empty regular fibre and dimension zero. This comparison at a supplied regular value is choice-free; it does not assert existence of regular values as an additional premise-free conclusion.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Write $d_M=(\\sin k_1,\\sin k_2,M+\\cos k_1+\\cos k_2)$. The band energies are $\\pm|d_M|$, so the gap is $2|d_M|$ and closes exactly if all three components vanish. The first two vanish only when each $k_j$ is $0$ or $\\pi$ modulo $2\\pi$; the third component at $(0,0),(\\pi,0),(0,\\pi),(\\pi,\\pi)$ is respectively $M+2,M,M,M-2$. Thus a zero exists exactly at $M=-2,0,2$. For every other fixed $M$, compactness of the torus and continuity give $\\min_k|d_M(k)|>0$, so the two bands have a uniform gap. The sine and cosine terms are finite Fourier sums with nearest-neighbor support, hence this is a finite-range Hermitian Bloch matrix.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Fix $M$ outside the closing set and let $N=(0,0,1)\\in S^2$. The preimages of $N$ under $n_M=d_M/|d_M|$ are exactly the four candidate points above for which the third component is positive. Near such a point, use the oriented coordinates $(n_1,n_2)$ at $N$. The derivative matrix is diagonal with entries $\\cos k_1/d_3$ and $\\cos k_2/d_3$, where $d_3=M+\\cos k_1+\\cos k_2>0$; its determinant is nonzero. Therefore $N$ is a regular value, and the local orientation signs at $(0,0),(\\pi,0),(0,\\pi),(\\pi,\\pi)$, whenever present, are respectively $+1,-1,-1,+1$. By [F2], degree is the sum of the signs for those points with $d_3>0$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "If $M<-2$, no candidate has $d_3>0$ and the sum is $0$. If $-2<M<0$, only $(0,0)$ contributes, giving $+1$. If $0<M<2$, $(0,0),(\\pi,0),(0,\\pi)$ contribute $+1-1-1=-1$. If $M>2$, all four contribute $+1-1-1+1=0$. Substitution into [F1] gives every table entry. For any two values in one of the four intervals, the closed segment between them remains in that interval; the joint function $|d_M(k)|$ is positive on the compact torus times that segment, so it has a positive minimum and supplies a gap-preserving deformation.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "step 2.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "M ranges over R; at M=−2,0,2 the example explicitly excludes a Chern number for the closing bands. See the item Statement or Definition."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "At M>2, the four north-pole signs sum to zero and both Chern numbers vanish. See the item Statement or Definition."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The lower and upper bands are rank one and have opposite Chern numbers. See the item Statement or Definition."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "At each nonclosing M, the two energies ±|dM| are distinct; degeneracies occur exactly at the listed closures. See the item Statement or Definition."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Each parameter interval is open; any closed segment between two parameters inside it has a positive uniform gap. See the item Statement or Definition."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "AC and CC are explicit for the Chern-degree supplier; the signed preimage count is finite. See the item Statement or Definition."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement is a direct definition or result, not an if-and-only-if claim."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement is a direct definition or result, not an if-and-only-if claim."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
          "check": "Take M=3.",
          "result": "All four north-pole preimages are present with signs +1,−1,−1,+1; their sum is zero and both Chern numbers vanish.",
          "verifies": "Numbered steps: 1.1, 2.1, 3.1."
        }
      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "beta-batch-54-author",
        "notes": "Checked the exact interfaces and assumptions, curvature and orientation signs, degenerate and boundary cases, and the conditional effective-model scope. This is authoring evidence, not independent proof approval."
      }
